## Derive

`derive` prints its own findings, not validation findings: drift (DRV-001, DRV-002), which `derive --write` applies, or report-only (DRV-003), which needs a person or `reconcile`. It exits 1 while unapplied drift remains, 2 when `tech-spec/` is absent, and 0 otherwise.

| Id | Kind | Meaning |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| DRV-001 | drift | A stack pin differs from the one version the lockfile locks for that package. `--write` sets the pin to the lockfile version and, when the pin's evidence is `repository` evidence citing a lockfile, refreshes the evidence version and date. |
| DRV-002 | drift | A guidelines layout path's `exists` flag disagrees with the tree. `--write` sets `exists: false` on a missing path and removes `exists: false` from a present one. |
| DRV-003 | report | A stack pin is absent from its ecosystem's lockfile or matches none of several versions the lockfile locks for that package (which one the pin means is a judgment, so it is never rewritten), or a verification command marked `exists: false` resolves (reconcile flips it when its owner archives). |

## Plan checks

`check-plan <file>` prints these for an epic plan file, which lives outside `.constitution/`. It validates the front matter against `epic-plan.schema.json` and cross-checks it against the constitution under `--root`; milestone bodies are prose and stay unchecked. Every finding is an error, and any error exits 1.

The required `kind` decides what the plan executes. `epic` plans name `EPIC-<ID>` and an `epic_file` that exists and matches. `fast_lane` plans (a code-only fix) and `no_constitution` plans (an issue in a repository with no `.constitution/`) name their work `FAST-<slug>` or `ISSUE-<number>` and set `epic_file: none`. A `fast_lane` plan is still cross-checked against the register and the active epics, so `--root` must have a `.constitution/`. A `no_constitution` plan skips PLAN-002, PLAN-003, and PLAN-004, and `--root` may lack `.constitution/`. The optional `invocation` and `reasoning_effort` fields are described under Plan authority in `SKILL.md`.

{% if harness.id == "claude" %}A bundle is one file holding a whole wave's plans, because in plan mode the wave lead can write only one file.{% elif harness.id == "codex" or harness.id == "antigravity" %}A bundle is one file holding a whole wave's plans for step-0 review.{% else %}A bundle, one file holding a whole wave's plans, is optional; plan mode does not require it, and `check-plan` validates a bundle whenever a file has markers.{% endif %} Each plan starts at a line `<!-- plan: EPIC-XX -->` immediately followed by its own `---` front matter block, and runs to the next marker; text before the first marker is not checked. In a file with markers, each plan is checked on its own, with every finding prefixed by its id (`EPIC-C: ...`), and a marker not immediately followed by front matter is PLAN-000 for that plan; then the plans are checked against each other (PLAN-007). A file with no marker is checked as a single plan.

| Id | Severity | Meaning |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| PLAN-000 | error | The plan file is missing, its front matter does not parse, or it has none |
| PLAN-001 | error | The front matter violates `epic-plan.schema.json`: a key is missing, unknown (including `pr_review.rounds` and `pr_review.reviewer`), or out of range; or `epic` and `epic_file` do not fit `kind` (`EPIC-<ID>` and a path for `epic`; `FAST-<slug>` or `ISSUE-<number>` and `none` otherwise). The pointer names the field |
| PLAN-002 | error | For a `kind: epic` plan, `epic_file` does not exist or does not parse, or its id does not match `epic`. For a `fast_lane` plan, `--root` has no `.constitution/` |
| PLAN-003 | error | An `open_decisions` id is not in the register |
| PLAN-004 | error | A `siblings` entry is not an active epic |
| PLAN-005 | error | A self-planned or explicit `single_epic_wave` plan, whose invocation has no separate wave lead, has an autonomy entry with `escalate_wave_lead` |
| PLAN-006 | error | `autonomy.policy` does not hold exactly one entry per trigger in the schema's trigger enum (`blocker`, `open_decision`, `plan_drift`, `spec_drift`, `test_failure`, `review_nonconvergence`, `rebase_conflict`, `budget_threshold`, `stall`). One finding names the missing triggers and one names the repeated ones. Runs for every `kind` |
| PLAN-007 | error | Bundle only. Two markers name the same plan; a marker's id differs from its plan's `epic`; a `siblings[].epic` names the plan itself or no plan in the bundle, or its `merge_position` differs from that plan's; or two plans take the same `merge_position` |
| PLAN-008 | error | Explicit `invocation.mode` disagrees with `planned_by` (`standalone` requires `self`; `orchestrated` and `single_epic_wave` require `wave_lead`), or a `single_epic_wave` lacks `lanes_in_wave: 1`. |

## Glossary

| Id | Severity | Meaning |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| GLS-001 | error | A banned synonym appears on a domain-facing surface, as declared by `surfaces` in `constitution.yaml` |
| GLS-002 | info | Banned synonyms appear off-surface; general feedback only |
| GLS-003 | warning | A `surfaces` glob in `constitution.yaml` matches no file the scan sees, so GLS-001 checks nothing there. Matching is exactly GLS-001's, over the same repository walk, which skips `.git`, dependency, and build directories. A glob under `.constitution/<stage>/` is skipped while that stage directory does not exist, because `init` writes surfaces for later stages. |

The GLS-001 and GLS-002 scan skips the glossary itself, reports, research, spikes, completed epics, sidecars, and structured data files, so field names and history never count.
